::GEO_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Two similar figures have corresponding sides 5 and 20. What is the scale factor from first to second?
{
~A. 15#This subtracts instead of comparing by ratio.
=B. 4#20 divided by 5 equals 4.
~C. 25#This adds the side lengths.
~D. ¼#This is the reverse scale factor.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A similar triangle has scale factor 3. A side of 11 becomes what length?
{
~A. 14#Adding 3 does not use scale factor.
~B. 8#Subtracting 3 does not use scale factor.
~C. 3.67#Dividing by 3 scales in reverse.
=D. 33#Multiply 11 by 3.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A map scale is 1 inch to 25 miles. How far is 4 inches?
{
=A. 100 miles#Four inches represents 4 times 25 miles.
~B. 29 miles#Adding 4 and 25 does not use the scale.
~C. 6.25 miles#Dividing 25 by 4 reverses the relationship.
~D. 75 miles#That would match 3 inches, not 4.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A 5-ft person casts a 6-ft shadow. A tree casts an 18-ft shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the tree?
{
~A. 17 ft#Adding 12 to the height copies a difference rather than a ratio.
~B. 21.6 ft#Multiplying by 4.32 does not use the shadow scale factor.
=C. 15 ft#The shadow scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; 5 times 3 is 15.
~D. 10 ft#This does not match the scale factor of 3.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Two congruent figures are similar with what scale factor?
{
~A. 0#A scale factor of 0 collapses lengths.
=B. 1#Conguent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1.
~C. 2#A scale factor of 2 changes size.
~D. ½#A scale factor of ½ changes size.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A model car is 12 cm long at a 1:20 scale. What is the actual length?
{
=A. 240 cm#Actual length is 20 times the model length: 12 times 20.
~B. 32 cm#Adding 20 does not use the scale ratio.
~C. 8 cm#Subtracting 20 is not meaningful here.
~D. 0.6 cm#Dividing by 20 scales in the wrong direction.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A drawing uses scale factor ⅕. An actual length is 60 cm. What is the drawing length?
{
~A. 55 cm#Subtracting 5 does not use scale.
~B. 65 cm#Adding 5 does not use scale.
~C. 300 cm#This uses the reciprocal scale and scales up.
=D. 12 cm#60 times ⅕ equals 12.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
If corresponding sides are 7 to 14 and 9 to x, what is x?
{
~A. 16#Adding 7 repeats a difference rather than a scale.
~B. 11#Adding 2 does not use the scale factor.
=C. 18#The scale factor is 2, so 9 becomes 18.
~D. 4.5#Dividing by 2 scales in the wrong direction.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Which proportion matches corresponding sides 3 to 12 and 5 to x?
{
=A. 3/12 = 5/x#Corresponding sides must be placed in the same order in both ratios.
~B. 3/5 = 12/x#This compares sides from different figures in the first ratio.
~C. 3 + 12 = 5 + x#Similarity uses multiplicative ratios, not addition.
~D. 3x = 5#This omits the corresponding side length 12.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A 2-inch drawing length represents 18 feet. How many feet does 5 inches represent?
{
~A. 23 feet#Adding 5 to 18 does not use the scale.
~B. 36 feet#This would match 4 inches at 9 feet per inch.
~C. 7.2 feet#Dividing 36 by 5 does not match the scale relationship.
=D. 45 feet#The scale is 9 feet per inch, so 5 inches represents 45 feet.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A side of 16 corresponds to 10 in a smaller similar figure. What is the scale factor from larger to smaller?
{
~A. 6#This subtracts side lengths.
=B. 0.625#The scale factor is 10 divided by 16, or 0.625.
~C. 26#This adds side lengths.
~D. 1.6#This is the reverse scale factor from smaller to larger.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A similar polygon has scale factor 2.5. A 4-unit side becomes what length?
{
~A. 6.5 units#Adding 2.5 does not use scale factor.
~B. 1.6 units#Dividing by 2.5 scales in reverse.
=C. 10 units#4 times 2.5 equals 10.
~D. 8 units#This uses a scale factor of 2, not 2.5.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A 6-cm model represents 150 cm. What is the scale factor from model to actual?
{
~A. 144#This subtracts lengths instead of finding the ratio.
~B. 156#This adds lengths instead of finding the ratio.
~C. 0.04#This is the reverse scale factor from actual to model.
=D. 25#150 divided by 6 equals 25.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A shadow problem creates similar triangles. A 4-ft pole casts an 8-ft shadow; a building casts a 40-ft shadow. What is the building height?
{
=A. 20 ft#The shadow scale factor is 5, so 4 times 5 is 20.
~B. 36 ft#Subtracting or using the shadow difference does not preserve the ratio.
~C. 44 ft#Adding 40 and 4 does not use similar triangles.
~D. 10 ft#This uses only half the needed scale factor.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Two similar pentagons have scale factor 0.5 from P to Q. A side in P is 18. What is the corresponding side in Q?
{
~A. 18.5#Adding 0.5 does not use scale.
~B. 36#This uses the reciprocal and scales up.
=C. 9#18 times 0.5 equals 9.
~D. 17.5#Subtracting 0.5 does not use scale.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
What information is needed to set up a proportion for similar figures?
{
~A. Only the area color#Color is unrelated to proportional side lengths.
=B. Corresponding side lengths#Proportions compare matching corresponding sides.
~C. Only the number of vertices#The number of vertices does not determine side ratios.
~D. Only the page size#Page size does not provide corresponding lengths.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A scale factor from A to B is 4. A side in B is 52. What was the corresponding side in A?
{
~A. 56#Adding 4 does not reverse scale.
~B. 48#Subtracting 4 does not reverse scale.
=C. 13#Divide 52 by 4 to reverse the scale factor.
~D. 208#Multiplying by 4 scales from A to B again.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A similar rectangle has width 30 corresponding to original width 12. What is the scale factor?
{
=A. 2.5#30 divided by 12 equals 2.5.
~B. 18#This subtracts widths.
~C. 42#This adds widths.
~D. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A blueprint has a 3-inch wall representing 24 feet. What does 7 inches represent?
{
~A. 31 feet#Adding 7 and 24 does not use the scale.
~B. 72 feet#This would match 9 inches at the same scale.
~C. 3.43 feet#Dividing 24 by 7 does not use the unit rate correctly.
=D. 56 feet#The scale is 8 feet per inch, so 7 inches represents 56 feet.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
If x corresponds to 24 and 5 corresponds to 15, what is x?
{
~A. 12#This uses an incorrect scale factor.
=B. 8#The scale factor is 3, so x times 3 equals 24; x is 8.
~C. 34#Adding the known values is not proportional reasoning.
~D. 72#This multiplies 24 by 3 instead of reversing the scale.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Which first step best supports this lesson skill?
{
~A. Guess from the picture before checking labels.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. Identify the givens and the geometric object before choosing a relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Proportional Relationships.
~C. Skip directly to the final answer.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. Use a formula that is not connected to the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Which explanation best shows mastery of this lesson?
{
~A. The answer only states a number.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. The answer says the diagram looks correct.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. The answer copies vocabulary without using it.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=D. The answer names the evidence, applies the correct relationship, and interprets the conclusion.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Proportional Relationships.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
What should a student check before submitting work for this lesson?
{
=A. That labels, notation, units, and reasoning match the lesson relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Proportional Relationships.
~B. That every answer is longer than the choices.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. That no diagram or evidence is included.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. That the work uses a future unit topic.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Which error should be avoided in this lesson?
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. Explaining why a theorem applies.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=C. Using appearance or an unrelated formula instead of the evidence in the task.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Proportional Relationships.
~D. Checking the final answer in context.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L04_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Which response is most aligned to the lesson objective?
{
~A. A response using a topic from a later unit.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. A step-by-step solution for Proportional Relationships that cites the correct evidence.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Proportional Relationships.
~C. A response with no labels or explanation.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. A response that changes the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
